  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Pace Band",
    "overview": "The run screen is a runner's pace band: a laminated strip with one cobalt band holding the cadence and the ramp printed as split rows you read like splits. Everything below the band is white lamination ruled into tight modules, each under a small ink header tab seated on its top rule, the way a printed band or race card divides its fields. Measurements are always condensed tabular figures; words are Roboto. Hi-vis lime appears only where the runner is right now.\n\nDensity is packed and tabular rather than airy: modules sit 16dp apart, options are ruled cells in a grid, and the one large figure is the cadence in the band. Dark mode is the same band at night: ink ground, a deeper cobalt band, the same lime. The world rejects the category default of a centred number inside a progress ring on a tinted surface; the band is edge-to-edge and the ring is a thin beat marker, not a progress meter.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One edge-to-edge cobalt band under the status bar owns the first viewport.",
      "Lime marks \"you are here\" and nothing else.",
      "Hairline-ruled modules under ink header tabs, packed tight on a 20dp gutter.",
      "Condensed tabular figures for every measurement; Roboto for every word.",
      "Selection is inked solid, never tinted."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The You-Are-Here Rule",
        "body": "Lime marks the runner's present position (the current ramp split, the beat flash) and nothing else. No lime buttons, borders, icons or decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Band-Tinted Rule",
        "body": "Secondary text and rules on the band are cobalt-tinted (Band Muted), never neutral grey or white at reduced alpha over grey.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Figures Rule",
        "body": "Every number a runner reads (cadence, clock, steps, splits, preset values, slider values) is set in Barlow Condensed with `tnum`, so digits never jitter as they change. Words are never set in the figure face except the header tabs and the unit under the cadence.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Scale-Not-Squeeze Rule",
        "body": "When text grows, grids lose columns and rows stack; labels are never truncated or shrunk.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ruled-Not-Raised Rule",
